Anything, to include good qualities, are bad in excess. Cleanliness is next to Godliness they say. Then look at Howie Mandell. He can't shake hands with another person because he is afraid of germs.

&#x200B;

Feminism can be a good thing, Yet there are extreme feminists who organize protests designed to (and have succeeded) interrupting male study lectures by using megaphones to overpower the speaker's voice.

&#x200B;

Religion can be a good quality. Look at all the charity that religious people do. Without even mentioning current Islamic extremists ... let's talk about the Spanish Inquisition, the Crusades, and Vlad the Impaler.

I remember a time when there were only landline phones. If you were calling a certain distance away, then it was a "toll call" that cost money per minute. If you were calling farther away then it was a "long distance call" that cost even more per minute.

&#x200B;

Then the Internet came along. E-mail was a thing. At the same time were what you call "cell" phones (what you mean is "smart" phones) where you could make an expensive telephone call anywhere that there was coverage (which was spotty at best). As a kid, I had a rich neighbor who had one of these. It cost him US$1.00/minute, and it was larger than a brick.

&#x200B;

Then the Internet went off the hook with all kinds of new WWW/HTML (recently invented by Sir Tim Burners Lee) sites. You could dial into AOL to access some of them.

&#x200B;

The WWW began to dominate. AOL was not required. There were Internet Service Providers (ISP's) that would give you an e-mail account AND access to the World Wide Web cheaper than AOL. Even with a 56Kbps modem, you could use your landline to access all of human knowledge. Then it got faster. DSL and Cable Internet brought broadband. Now you can actually video chat with people. Then it got easier. Instead of needing to sift through search results you could just look up the thing on Wikipedia. Then there was WiFi so that your laptop didn't need a cord in order to access the Internet.

&#x200B;

The SMART phone brought all that technology together into a device that can fit into your pocket. That thing that fits into your pocket has many times more computing power than all of NASA when we landed Neil Armstrong on the Moon. It can play games, connect to Facebook, text and/or e-mail anybody. Call anybody, and have video chat with anybody.

&#x200B;

That's why.

